Q: How can I take the raw bits of an int32 and create a float32 from them? A: <p>You can easily write code to reinterpret the bits of the 32-bit <code>int</code> as a float, using either a <code>union</code> or <code>memcpy</code>, without any overhead:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdint.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;

static_assert(sizeof(float) == sizeof(int32_t), &quot;unsupported architecture: float and int32_t have different sizes&quot;);

float reinterpret_int32(int32_t x) {
    float f;
    memcpy(&amp;f, &amp;x, sizeof f);
    return f;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Note however these remarks:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><code>rand()</code> produces a uniformly distributed set of peudo-random numbers in the range <code>0</code> to <code>RAND_MAX</code>, which may be smaller than <code>INT_MAX</code>.</p>
</li>
<li><p>if <code>int</code> has 32 bits, in the best scenario, this may be 31 rand bits, but some platform define <code>RAND_MAX</code> to a much smaller value, as little as <code>32767</code>, hence only 15 random bit. Even combining 2 random values may produce less than 32 random bits.</p>
</li>
<li><p>converting these bits as a <code>float</code> will produce random floating point values with a very different range and distribution, eg: only positive values or only in a very small range of very small values.</p>
</li>
<li><p>a sizeable proportion of the <code>float</code> values produced will be NaNs, which you should not use in computations, especially if they are signaling NaNs which may cause floating point exceptions to be triggered.</p>
</li>
<li><p>the size of <code>float</code> and <code>int</code> might even differ, albeit unlikely, as well the bit ordering.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>If you want a uniformly distributed set of <code>float</code> values in a given range, you should use a division:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;

float rand_float(float low, float high) {
    return low + (high - low) * rand() / RAND_MAX;
}
</code></pre>
<p>If <code>double</code> is fast on your target system, you will get better precision with:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;

float rand_float(float low, float high) {
    return (float)(low + (double)(high - low) * rand() / RAND_MAX);
}
</code></pre>
